To calculate the total amount of resources traded, we need to simulate the interactions between the robots and keep track of the resources exchanged during trades. Here's a possible implementation in MATLAB:
```
% Generate random resources for each robot
robot_resources = randi([1, 10], 10, 1);
% Initialize variables
alliances = zeros(10, 1);
resources_traded = 0;
% Loop over all pairs of robots
for i = 1:10
 for j = i+1:10
 % Check if robots form an alliance
 if robot_attributes(i, 1) > 0.5 && robot_attributes(j, 1) > 0.5
 alliances(i) = j;
 alliances(j) = i;
 end

 % Check if robots engage in conflict
 if robot_attributes(i, 2) > 0.5 && robot_attributes(j, 2) > 0.5
 if robot_resources(i) > robot_resources(j)
 resources_traded = resources_traded + robot_resources(j);
 robot_resources(i) = robot_resources(i) + robot_resources(j);
 robot_resources(j) = 0;
 else
 resources_traded = resources_traded + robot_resources(i);
 robot_resources(j) = robot_resources(j) + robot_resources(i);
 robot_resources(i) = 0;
 end
 end

 % Check if robots trade resources
 if alliances(i) == j && robot_attributes(i, 3) > 0.5 && robot_attributes(j, 3) > 0.5
 resources_traded = resources_traded + min(robot_resources(i), robot_resources(j));
 robot_resources(i) = robot_resources(i) - resources_traded;
 robot_resources(j) = robot_resources(j) + resources_traded;
 end
 end
end
% Print results
num_alliances = sum(alliances > 0);
fprintf('Number of alliances formed: %dn', num_alliances);
fprintf('Total amount of resources traded: %dn', resources_traded);
```
Note that this implementation assumes that each robot can form at most one alliance with another robot, and that conflicts and trades only happen between pairs of robots that have formed an alliance. This may not be the case in a more complex simulation, but it should be enough to illustrate the basic logic of the problem.

